# One type per file

Every top-level `class`, `record`, `struct`, `interface`, and `enum` goes in its own `.cs` file named after the type. Do not dump catalogs, contracts, or view-models into a shared file.

```csharp
// BAD — Catalog.cs or Contracts.cs
public abstract class Entity { }
public class City : Entity { }
public interface IAuthService { }
public enum PermissionAction { View = 4 }

// GOOD
// Entity.cs, City.cs, IAuthService.cs, PermissionAction.cs
namespace Hma.Domain.Entities;

public class City : Entity
{
    public string Code { get; set; } = "";
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";
}
```

Allowed exceptions:

- `partial` type split by WPF: `CustomerView.xaml` + `CustomerView.xaml.cs` (still one type).
- A single `file`-scoped or `private` nested type used only as an implementation detail of the outer type.
- One static `DependencyInjection` class per project in `DependencyInjection.cs`.

When adding a type, create the file in the matching folder (`Entities/`, `Services/`, `Abstractions/`, `ViewModels/`, `Views/`). When touching a file that already contains multiple types, split them before adding more. Do not put ViewModels for different screens in `*ViewModels.cs`.
